{{/*
  Targets to delete on apply. chezmoi does not remove a file just because it
  left the source tree — it stops managing it — so anything being retired needs
  naming here to actually leave the machines it already reached.

  ── This whole file is temporary ────────────────────────────────────────────
  Every entry is a one-shot cleanup, and each is a no-op the moment the machine
  it names has applied once. chezmoi keeps no record of "already removed": the
  list is declarative, re-asserting absence on every apply, which is why an
  entry looks permanent when it is only *pending*.

  Delete the whole file once **both** machines have applied — the Linux one
  included, which as of 2026-08-27 has not, so all four below are still doing
  work there and none here.

  Entries are written in the past tense on purpose: each describes the thing
  that used to exist and why it went, not a hazard that is still live.
*/ -}}

{{/*
  bin/regenerate-completions wrote _just and _chezmoi into brew's
  site-functions. Every completion there is a symlink brew maintains into the
  Cellar, so running it downgraded a self-updating completion to a static file
  frozen at whatever version generated it. Nothing replaced it; there was
  nothing to generate.
*/ -}}
bin/regenerate-completions

{{/*
  plugins/tmux-navigator.lua declared the Neovim half of vim-tmux-navigator. Its
  four <C-hjkl> mappings moved to config/keymaps.lua as plain wincmd, and the
  plugin went with the tmux half.

  It needed naming here rather than just deleting from the source tree, because
  the two steps do different things. Dropping the source entry stopped chezmoi
  managing the target; the file stayed on disk, lazy.nvim went on reading every
  lua/plugins/*.lua it found, and so the spec was still declared — the plugin
  stayed installed and its lazy key stubs went on shadowing the new mappings.
  This line is what deleted the file. With no spec left, nothing reinstalls it.
*/ -}}
.config/nvim/lua/plugins/tmux-navigator.lua

{{/*
  .config/bat/themes held a 64KB Catppuccin Mocha .tmTheme. Three separate
  reasons it did nothing: bat only loads a custom theme after `bat cache
  --build` and no cache was ever built, bat 0.26 ships all four Catppuccin
  themes built in, and the config asks for Frappe, not Mocha.
*/ -}}
.config/bat/themes

{{/*
  bin/claude-window opened Claude as a full-terminal tmux window on prefix + C,
  alongside the in-editor integration rather than instead of it. Only one of the
  two was used: claudecode.nvim's float, which keeps the selection, the
  @-mention keys and the diff view that a bare terminal has none of. The tmux
  binding went with it.
*/ -}}
bin/claude-window
